20:30:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair—and painfully accurate—way to put it. The official, well-thought-out plan was always **IPv6**, designed in the 1990s to be the clean, permanent fix. The plan was *not* to pile NAT on top of NAT forever. That happened because the transition turned out to be far messier and slower than anyone expected. Here’s the honest breakdown of how we ended up in that situation: --- ### The real plan (ideal world) 1. **Design IPv6** in the mid-’90s, with enough addresses for everything forever. 2. **Deploy dual-stack** everywhere, so every device speaks both IPv4 and IPv6. 3. **Phase out IPv4** once everyone had migrated. That was the genuine roadmap. No one ever sat down and said, “Let’s just keep adding more layers of NAT.” --- ### Why the plan fell apart The migration hit several unanticipated roadblocks: - **Network effect / chicken-and-egg:** ISPs and content providers each waited for the other to move first. If your favourite sites weren’t on IPv6, you didn’t need it. If your customers didn’t have IPv6, you didn’t bother supporting it. - **Hardware and software lag:** Old consumer routers, enterprise gear, and embedded systems didn’t support IPv6 well (or at all). Replacing everything takes decades. - **NAT bought too much time:** Network Address Translation (NAT) worked well enough for most people. It turned 4.3 billion addresses into many billions of usable sockets through port mapping. So the pain of shortage was delayed—killing the urgency. - **Lack of backward compatibility:** IPv4 and IPv6 are separate protocols. They don’t talk to each other without translation, making a smooth, gradual switch hard. Compare that to the Y2K problem, which was just a data fix on the same protocol. So instead of a swift cutover, the internet did what complex systems do: it **adapted with a pile of tactical hacks** that gave us more time. --- ### The “slap NAT on top of NAT” phase That’s exactly what happened: 1. **Home/office NAT** (one public IP per site) was already standard. This absorbed the first wave of device growth. 2. When even those addresses ran out, ISPs deployed **Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT)**, sharing a single public IP among hundreds of subscribers. That’s NAT on top of the subscriber’s existing NAT. (Cue the CGNAT complaints about double NAT, broken port forwarding, etc.) 3. Then came **NAT64/DNS64**—translation mechanisms so IPv6-only clients can reach IPv4 servers. Yet more translation layers. None of this was envisioned as the primary solution. It was a coping mechanism for the failed timeline. --- ### Has the plan really worked out? Eventually, yes—but not because of a top-down coordinated switch. IPv6 adoption eventually hit critical mass thanks to two huge drivers: - **Mobile networks:** When LTE rolled out, carriers made IPv6 mandatory because they had far too many devices and no public IPv4 per phone. So they went IPv6-only with NAT64 to reach the IPv4 world. This forced apps and services to work over IPv6. - **Large content providers:** Google, Facebook, Netflix, YouTube, and others turned on IPv6. Once those are dual-stacked, an ISP turning on IPv6 instantly sees a massive chunk of traffic shift, making the business case. So today, ~40%+ of global traffic already flows over IPv6, mostly from mobile and big platforms. Many ISPs in fast-growing regions (India, Brazil, parts of Europe) are now deploying IPv6-first. CGNAT is still everywhere, but gradually the internet is going dual-stack, and in some places IPv6-only. --- ### So were they right to “just hope IPv6 works out”? In hindsight, it looks like a bet that is *slowly* paying off—but the industry paid for the delay with two decades of messy NAT layering. The original designers probably underestimated how hard it is to replace a running global protocol without a flip-of-a-switch flag day. To directly answer your summarised statement: Yes, the plan was to transition to IPv6, but the transition was so slow that the *practical half-measure* became a ever-growing stack of NATs, which we’re still living with. It’s not that they intended it to be the plan; it’s just what reality forced on them.
